.TH CHMAP_CONSTRUCT_MP 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_construct_mp \- declare and create a hash map that takes its memory from a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_construct_mp(hm_name, key_t, val_t, mmgmt_procs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a hash map handle named hm_name, along with the companion type
variables that pass key_t and val_t on to every later type-inferred
macro, and creates a map that takes all of its memory from mmgmt_procs.

It is chmap_construct(3) with an allocator added, and the map uses its
own hashing for key_t. chmap_construct_ch(3) swaps the hashing instead,
chmap_construct_full(3) takes both, and chmap_construct_mp_scoped(3) is
this macro with a cleanup attribute that destroys the map at scope exit.

The two-step form is chmap_declare(3) followed by chmap_init_mp(3). Use
it when a creation failure has to be handled instead of being fatal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name      Name for the map handle to declare
key_t        Key type; it tells every later macro how to read a key and,
             with val_t, which implementation strategy the map uses
val_t        Value type
mmgmt_procs  Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the default
             allocator
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro declares variables and is not an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program through _ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
All four members of ccol_memmgmt_procs_t must be non-NULL
The macro copies the procedures into the map, so your own
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t doesn't need to outlive the call, but the
procedures themselves must stay callable for the whole life of the map
Everything the map allocates later, its bucket array included, comes
from these procedures, and everything it frees goes back to them
Uses CCOL_DEFAULT_INITIAL_BUCKET_ARRAY_SIZE (16) buckets
chmap_destroy(3) destroys the map. The companion type variables live in
the scope of the handle, so a function that receives the handle needs
chmap_redeclare(3) inside it
An array key or value type, such as int[2] or char[16], is a compile
error, and the message says to wrap the array in a struct. The macros copy
a key or a value by assignment and C cannot assign an array, so the map
would store the address of the caller's array instead of its elements. A
struct that holds the array works
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};

chmap_construct_mp(scores, char *, int, &procs);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_construct (3),
.BR chmap_construct_mp_scoped (3),
.BR chmap_construct_ch (3),
.BR chmap_construct_full (3),
.BR chmap_init_mp (3),
.BR chmap_declare (3),
.BR chmap_redeclare (3),
.BR chmap_create_full (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3)
